      <article id="packaging-mistakes" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Release creation</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Packaging mistakes</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            Sometimes, the original packager would have a queue of tasks and would occasionally pack and include the wrong text files in the release.
            In the era of MS-DOS without application multitasking, this was an easy mistake to make.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="bbs-mistakes" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Distribution</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">BBS mistakes</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            During the peak BBS era of the 1990s, many bulletin boards utilized scripts and automated tools to help manage the influx of uploads.
            At times, they would inject advertising into the files, parse or replace metadata, or rebuild the release archive from scratch.
            However, these scripts could be buggy and break the packaged release before it got reshared elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="oldware-abandonware" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Repackaging</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Oldware and abandonware communities</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            It might be surprising today, but even in the mid-1990s, there was an active online community of people who were into old and retro gaming.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the time, the web was still a relatively new technology and wasn't the most effective way to share files.
            People would instead share online over FTP servers (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File_Transfer_Protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">File Transfer Protocol</a>)
            or using IRC (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Internet Relay Chat</a>) chatbots with the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XDCC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">XDCC</a> file exchange protocol, with <code>#oldwarez</code> being the primary chat channel.
          </p>
          <p>
            However, the Scene also used FTP servers and IRC, with some groups even hosting new-release warez over IRC chatbots.
            <a href="/g/fast-action-trading-elite">fATE</a>'s <code>#fatefiles</code> and <a href="/g/motiv8">Motiv8</a>'s <code>#m8leech</code> were two notable chat channel examples.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is speculation, yet the close online proximity of new-release pirated software probably made some members of the abandonware community anxious.
            They were sharing copyrighted software online but felt justified as their titles were out of print and unattainable.
            Yet they did not want to be associated with the pirates of contemporary software or draw the ire of law enforcement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many in the abandonware community would reshare old Scene releases but modify or
            delete the included texts by removing references to the original Scene group or
            deleting the listings of the distribution boards and group members.
            It was an issue acknowledged at the time with the 1996 group Old Warez Inc <a href="/f/af4624">writing</a>,
            <q>We DO NOT STRIP ANY CREDIT FROM THESE ORIGINAL RELEASERS</q>.
            Unfortunately, these tainted Scene releases are sometimes the ones that survive today.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-top mt-3 pt-3 small text-body-secondary">
          <span class="fw-semibold text-body me-1">Examples:</span>
          <span class="font-monospace">(1995-96)</span> <a href="/g/distributors-of-classic-warez">Distributors of Classic Warez</a>,
          <span class="font-monospace">(1996)</span> <a href="/g/dead-pirates-society">Dead Pirates Society</a>,
          <span class="font-monospace">(1996-97)</span> <a href="/g/8088-state">8088 State</a>,
          <span class="font-monospace">(1996-98)</span> <a href="/g/old-warez-inc">Old Warez Inc</a>,
          <span class="font-monospace">(1998)</span> <a href="/g/classic-old-warez">Classic Old Warez</a>,
          <a href="/f/ab287ee">BloodRose XDCC</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="web-mistakes" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">Character encoding</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Web and encoding mistakes</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            English-language text files of the DOS and early Windows eras were predominantly saved in a limited character encoding known as
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_page_437" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code Page 437</a> (CP-437 or OEM-US),
            while microcomputers such as the Commodore Amiga and workstations running Unix used an incompatible character encoding,
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO/IEC_8859-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISO-8859-1</a> (also known as Latin-1).
          </p>
          <p>
            For a decade or two, ISO-8859-1 was also the default encoding used for the Internet, the web, and many web browsers.
            This meant <a href="/files/nfo" rel="nofollow">NFO</a> files created in CP-437 could not be correctly viewed in web browsers and could become corrupted if downloaded incorrectly.
            Today, when a text file using a legacy encoding such as CP-437 is saved or converted in the wrong encoding, the characters are often altered or lost and cannot be recovered.
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, the Defacto2 website hero text-logo was drawn in CP-437.
          </p>
        </div>

